"GreenPoint Maintenance Services Corp\",\"url\":\"https://greenpointms.com\"},\"mainEntityOfPage\":\"https://greenpointms.com/blog/what-is-atp-bioluminescence-testing-cleaning/\",\"keywords\":\"ATP bioluminescence testing, ATP testing cleaning, cleaning verification technology\"}"}}],["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"BreadcrumbList\",\"itemListElement\":[{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":1,\"name\":\"Home\",\"item\":\"https://greenpointms.com\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":2,\"name\":\"Blog\",\"item\":\"https://greenpointms.com/blog/\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":3,\"name\":\"What Is ATP Bioluminescence Testing and Why It Matters for Your Facility\"}]}"}}],["$","$L2",null,{}],["$","section",null,{"style":{"background":"#0A2A16","paddingTop":140,"paddingBottom":64,"position":"relative","overflow":"hidden"},"children":[["$","div",null,{"style":{"position":"absolute","inset":0,"opacity":0.04,"backgroundImage":"linear-gradient(rgba(255,255,255,0.1) 1px, transparent 1px), linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(255,255,255,0.1) 1px, transparent 1px)","backgroundSize":"60px 60px","pointerEvents":"none"}}],["$","div",null,{"style":{"maxWidth":720,"margin":"0 auto","padding":"0 24px","position":"relative","zIndex":1},"children":[["$","nav",null,{"style":{"display":"flex","gap":8,"alignItems":"center","marginBottom":24,"flexWrap":"wrap"},"children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/","style":{"fontFamily":"'JetBrains Mono', monospace","fontSize":11,"color":"rgba(255,255,255,0.4)","textDecoration":"none","fontWeight":600,"letterSpacing":"0.05em"},"children":"Home"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"rgba(255,255,255,0.2)","fontSize":12},"children":"›"}],["$","a",null,{"href":"/blog/","style":{"fontFamily":"'JetBrains Mono', monospace","fontSize":11,"color":"rgba(255,255,255,0.4)","textDecoration":"none","fontWeight":600,"letterSpacing":"0.05em"},"children":"Blog"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"rgba(255,255,255,0.2)","fontSize":12},"children":"›"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"fontFamily":"'JetBrains Mono', monospace","fontSize":11,"color":"#C8A34D","fontWeight":600,"letterSpacing":"0.05em"},"children":"Technology"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"style":{"display":"flex","alignItems":"center","gap":12,"marginBottom":20,"flexWrap":"wrap"},"children":[["$","span",null,{"style":{"background":"rgba(200,163,77,0.15)","color":"#C8A34D","padding":"4px 10px","borderRadius":4,"fontSize":11,"fontWeight":600,"fontFamily":"'JetBrains Mono', monospace","letterSpacing":"0.05em"},"children":"Technology"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"fontSize":12,"color":"rgba(255,255,255,0.4)","fontFamily":"'JetBrains Mono', monospace"},"children":"November 1, 2025"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"fontSize":12,"color":"rgba(255,255,255,0.4)","fontFamily":"'JetBrains Mono', monospace"},"children":["· ","6 min read"]}]]}],["$","h1",null,{"style":{"fontFamily":"'Syne', sans-serif","fontSize":"clamp(28px, 4vw, 42px)","fontWeight":700,"color":"#fff","lineHeight":1.15,"marginBottom":0,"letterSpacing":"-0.02em"},"children":"What Is ATP Bioluminescence Testing and Why It Matters for Your Facility"}]]}]]}],"$undefined",["$","article",null,{"style":{"maxWidth":720,"margin":"0 auto","padding":"48px 24px 64px"},"children":[[["$","p","0",{"style":{"fontSize":18,"color":"#1A2B1F","lineHeight":1.8,"marginBottom":32,"fontWeight":500},"children":"For decades, facility managers had only two ways to evaluate cleaning quality: visual inspection and complaint volume. Both are unreliable. A surface can look clean while harboring dangerous levels of bacteria, and complaints only surface after problems become obvious. ATP bioluminescence testing changes this equation entirely by providing instant, quantifiable measurements of surface cleanliness. Here's how it works and why it matters."}],["$","h2","1",{"style":{"fontFamily":"'Syne', sans-serif","fontSize":22,"fontWeight":700,"color":"#1A2B1F","lineHeight":1.25,"marginTop":40,"marginBottom":16,"letterSpacing":"-0.01em"},"children":"How ATP Testing Works"}],["$","p","2",{"style":{"fontSize":16,"color":"#4A5E52","lineHeight":1.8,"marginBottom":20},"children":"ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is the energy molecule present in all living cells — including bacteria, viruses, mold, and organic residue. An ATP bioluminescence test uses a simple swab to collect a surface sample, which is then inserted into a luminometer. The device measures light produced by a chemical reaction with the ATP molecules present. The result is a numerical reading in RLUs (Relative Light Units) that indicates the amount of biological contamination on the surface. Higher RLUs mean more contamination; lower RLUs mean a cleaner surface."}],["$","h2","3",{"style":{"fontFamily":"'Syne', sans-serif","fontSize":22,"fontWeight":700,"color":"#1A2B1F","lineHeight":1.25,"marginTop":40,"marginBottom":16,"letterSpacing":"-0.01em"},"children":"What the Numbers Mean"}],["$","p","4",{"style":{"fontSize":16,"color":"#4A5E52","lineHeight":1.8,"marginBottom":20},"children":"While acceptable thresholds vary by surface type and facility, general guidelines for commercial environments are: readings under 10 RLUs indicate hospital-grade cleanliness, 10-25 RLUs represent excellent commercial cleaning, 26-50 RLUs suggest acceptable but improvable conditions, and readings above 50 RLUs indicate inadequate cleaning requiring immediate attention. For healthcare environments, thresholds are typically stricter, with most facilities targeting under 25 RLUs for patient contact surfaces."}],["$","h2","5",{"style":{"fontFamily":"'Syne', sans-serif","fontSize":22,"fontWeight":700,"color":"#1A2B1F","lineHeight":1.25,"marginTop":40,"marginBottom":16,"letterSpacing":"-0.01em"},"children":"Why Visual Inspection Fails"}],["$","p","6",{"style":{"fontSize":16,"color":"#4A5E52","lineHeight":1.8,"marginBottom":20},"children":"Studies consistently show that visual inspection correctly identifies surface cleanliness only 30-40% of the time. A desk can look perfectly clean while registering 200+ RLUs of biological contamination. Conversely, a slightly dusty surface might register under 10 RLUs. This disconnect is why healthcare facilities, food processing plants, and pharmaceutical companies have adopted ATP testing as a standard verification method — and why commercial facilities are increasingly following suit."}],["$","h2","7",{"style":{"fontFamily":"'Syne', sans-serif","fontSize":22,"fontWeight":700,"color":"#1A2B1F","lineHeight":1.25,"marginTop":40,"marginBottom":16,"letterSpacing":"-0.01em"},"children":"Implementing ATP Testing in Your Facility"}],["$","p","8",{"style":{"fontSize":16,"color":"#4A5E52","lineHeight":1.8,"marginBottom":20},"children":"ATP testing is most valuable when used systematically rather than randomly. Best practices include testing high-touch surfaces on a regular schedule, establishing baseline readings before and after cleaning protocol changes, creating trend data to identify areas needing additional attention, sharing results with cleaning staff for accountability and training, and documenting readings for compliance and quality assurance purposes."}],["$","div","9",{"style":{"background":"#F0F7F2","borderLeft":"3px solid #1B7A3D","padding":"20px 24px","borderRadius":"0 8px 8px 0","margin":"32px 0"},"children":["$","p",null,{"style":{"fontSize":15,"color":"#1A2B1F","lineHeight":1.7,"fontWeight":500,"margin":0},"children":"GreenPoint integrates ATP bioluminescence testing into our JaniTrack verification system. Every reading is documented, timestamped, and available in your facility's online dashboard — giving you objective proof of cleaning quality anytime you need it."}]}]],["$","div",null,{"style":{"borderTop":"1px solid #E2EBE5","marginTop":48,"paddingTop":32,"display":"flex","flexDirection":"column","gap":16},"children":[["$","div",null,{"style":{"display":"flex","alignItems":"center","gap":14},"children":[["$"